NAME

say - Write joined text to standard output

SYNOPSIS

    say [-e|--escapes] [-n|--no-newline] [--] [text ...]
    say --help

DESCRIPTION

`say` writes its text operands to standard output, separated by one space. It
appends a newline unless `-n` or `--no-newline` is selected. With no text
operands it writes only the optional newline. Backslash translation is off by
default so ordinary and untrusted text remains literal.

OPTIONS

    -e, --escapes — Translate the supported backslash escapes before writing.
    -n, --no-newline — Do not append a final newline.
    -h, --help — Show concise usage and option help, then exit.
    -- — End option parsing; later arguments are text operands.

Grouped short flags work in either order: `-ne` and `-en` both enable escape
translation and suppress the final newline. Long options may be combined in
separate arguments.

ESCAPES

With `-e`, these backslash escapes are translated:

    \\ — Emit one literal backslash.
    \n — Emit a line feed.
    \r — Emit a carriage return.
    \t — Emit a tab.
    \e — Emit ASCII ESC byte 0x1b.

Unknown escapes and a final backslash remain literal, including both bytes of
an unknown pair. Without `-e`, all backslashes and the following characters
remain unchanged.

The ESC byte can introduce Mangrove terminal controls in the canonical
`ESC{command}` form. `\e` is only the spelling recognized by `say -e`; it is
not itself a terminal instruction. The supported commands are:

    fg:COLOR — Set foreground color.
    bg:COLOR — Set background color.
    warn — Apply warning style.
    err — Apply error style.
    ok — Apply success style.
    head — Apply heading style.
    reset — Restore default foreground and background.
    cls — Clear the screen.
    cll — Clear the current line.
    cleol — Clear from the cursor to the line end.
    at:ROW,COL — Move to one-based screen coordinates.
    up:N — Move up by N rows.
    down:N — Move down by N rows.
    left:N — Move left by N columns.
    right:N — Move right by N columns.
    hide — Hide the cursor.
    show — Show the cursor.
    alt — Enter the alternate screen.
    main — Return to the main screen.

`COLOR` is one of the existing palette names:
`black`, `red`, `green`, `yellow`, `blue`, `magenta`, `cyan`, `l_gray`,
`d_gray`, `b_red`, `b_green`, `b_yellow`, `b_blue`, `b_magenta`, `b_cyan`,
or `white`. Both foreground and background accept all 16 names. The color
values are Mangrove's existing palette; names do not select RGB values.

Unknown, malformed, oversized, or incomplete terminal commands have no effect
and remain visible as text; the ESC byte itself is displayed as U+FFFD. The
terminal validates a complete command before applying it. Only an actual ESC
byte followed by a recognized command is interpreted; printable `{fg:red}`
text has no special meaning.

EXAMPLES

    say Hello, Mangrove!
    say -n Loading...
    say -e '\e{fg:b_green}Success!\e{reset}'
    say -ne '\e{cls}\e{at:1,1}'

NOTES

Output redirection receives exactly the bytes `say` emits, including any ESC
bytes produced by `-e`; a redirected file is not interpreted by the terminal.
When writing to the interactive console, a valid terminal command can move the
cursor, clear output, or switch screens. Do not pass untrusted text through
`-e` if it may contain terminal-control bytes. The no-newline option lets the
next prompt or output continue at the current cursor position.

